SYNTH-0001♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔Perez♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔National Minority Quality Forum♔♔♔♔♔♔National Minority Quality Forum Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎The National Minority Quality Forum strongly recommends the Department recognize that accelerating AI adoption without mandatory equity safeguards will deepen existing disparities in behavioral health care for Medicaid-enrolled communities.⏎⏎National Minority Quality Forum Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0002♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔King♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health♔♔♔♔♔♔The Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health advises HHS against accelerating clinical AI deployment without first mandating rigorous prospective validation, standardized bias audits, and independent post-implementation monitoring protocols.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0003♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Williams♔♔Rhode Island♔♔United States♔Dartmouth Geisel School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔The Geisel School of Medicine at Dartmouth writes to express concern that HHS acceleration framing systematically bypasses distributional accountability for workers and patients bearing disproportionate deployment risk.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0004♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Adams♔♔Virginia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My new doctor's office almost gave me a prescription for a medication I'm severely allergic to. It's in my chart. Has been for years. The computer flagged it as a recommendation anyway and if my daughter hadn't been sitting there asking questions I would have taken that paper to CVS and filled it. That's not a hypothetical risk, that happened to me in northern Virginia in February. And now I'm reading that HHS wants to know how to speed this up faster?⏎⏎⏎⏎I've also read about how pulse oximeters gave wrong oxygen readings for Black patients during covid, and about insurance AI turning down coverage way more often for specific demographics. Those are not edge cases, this is already happening. My concern is that HHS is asking how to accelerate adoption before anyone has proven this technology won't harm patients who don't fit whatever "average" the system learned from. I really think you need to tap the brakes and answer that question first before figuring out how to roll this out faster.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0005♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Baker♔♔Rhode Island♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My client Dorothea is 84 and lives in Cranston. An automated system flagged her for a condition she doesn't have and it took her daughter two weeks of phone calls to fix it. That's one person. Thinking about the fallout when these systems get applied to everyone really worries me. We already know about oxygen monitors giving false readings for non-white patients, computer models missing severe infections in sick folks, and insurance algorithms unjustly denying care to minority groups. Those are real problems happening right now. Plus, the folks suffering the consequences, like seniors and Medicaid recipients, never even realize a machine made the decision.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0006♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah♔Rodriguez♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My doctor of 22 YEARS retired because he couldn't take the paperwork anymore!! When does the government fix THAT instead of chasing the next shiny thing?? AI flagged something real for my coworker Marcus but his doctor still had to interpret it and ACT on it — you can't automate that relationship away.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0007♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Thompson♔♔Texas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew Thompson⏎⏎4817 Crestwood Drive⏎⏎San Antonio, TX 78229⏎⏎⏎⏎October 14, 2024⏎⏎⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎⏎Back in 2022, the oncologist treating my wife ran her imaging through an AI screening tool and it flagged a secondary site. That flag turned out to be a false positive. She spent six weeks terrified, and when I asked the oncology staff how the system had reached that conclusion, nobody could tell me. Not in a "we'd rather not say" way. In a genuine "we don't know" way. The system had rendered a confident output, delivered with the same weight as a clinical finding, and the mechanism behind it was invisible to everyone in that room, including the people treating my wife.⏎⏎⏎⏎I want to be clear that I don't think the oncologist was negligent. I think she was handed a tool whose limitations were not disclosed to her, and that is the problem I want HHS to think about before it decides the answer to this moment is to go faster.⏎⏎⏎⏎Because here is what I've learned since then. I've done a fair amount of reading, more than I ever expected to do as an office manager from San Antonio. And the pattern I keep finding is not new. This is what health IT does. The EHR rollouts of the 2010s promised transformed care and delivered documentation burdens so severe that physician burnout became a public health conversation. Sepsis prediction algorithms were deployed at major hospital systems and later found to perform dramatically worse in real patients than in the validation studies. I read about research showing that clinical AI tools routinely degrade when they move from the benchmark dataset they were trained on into actual hospital environments, with different patient populations, different documentation habits, different equipment. The gap between the demo and the deployment is not a minor implementation wrinkle. It is a structural, recurring feature of this entire category of technology.⏎⏎⏎⏎And that gap matters more, not less, because of something called automation bias. When clinicians see a confident AI output, they trust it. Doctors naturally let their guard down. That means the system's mistakes are most likely to go unnoticed precisely when the AI is most wrong, which is when it's operating outside the conditions it was built for. Real clinical environments, with fragmented records and inconsistently coded diagnoses and patients whose conditions are changing faster than any dataset can track.⏎⏎⏎⏎My wife's false positive is, in some ways, a best-case scenario. They caught it. It was just terror and time. What happens when the miss goes the other direction?⏎⏎⏎⏎The crucial point the agency must recognize is that this RFI is framed entirely around acceleration. How do we speed up adoption. How do we reduce barriers. I genuinely cannot find anywhere in the framing an acknowledgment that moving faster with inadequately validated tools means real patients absorb the errors.⏎⏎⏎⏎Stop treating patients as the beta testing population for technology that hasn't earned clinical trust. Require that AI tools demonstrate real-world performance data from actual deployment environments before they reach patients, not just controlled validation studies. Require that every AI output in a clinical setting carry an explainability mechanism that a physician can actually use.⏎⏎⏎⏎The confidence interval on my wife's false positive was never shared with her doctor. I'd like to know why that's acceptable.⏎⏎⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎⏎⏎Matthew Thompson♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0008♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Martinez♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔Imagen Technologies♔♔♔♔♔♔Imagen Technologies urges HHS to reframe this RFI around protecting the human core of medicine before optimizing throughput, because treating patients in pain as mere statistical inputs inevitably leads to measurable clinical harm.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0009♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Miller♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I am certainly not claiming that artificial intelligence has zero chance of helping our rural clinics, such as the one in Minot that my daughter Cassie depended on. It might. My worry is that the first question a hospital administrator asks is how many nursing positions this justifies cutting, and the nurse left standing doesn't have time to question what the screen says.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0010♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel♔Torres♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔UC San Diego School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔The UC San Diego School of Medicine offers this perspective out of concern that accelerating AI clinical adoption before resolving accountability gaps systematically insulates every actor from responsibility when patients are harmed.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0011♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Torres♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔American Speech-Language-Hearing Association♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Speech-Language-Hearing Association recommends that the Department tie all artificial intelligence adoption incentives to the proven preservation of the human clinical relationship. Individuals within communication-impaired populations must never be treated merely as algorithmic inputs.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0012♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Allen♔♔Tennessee♔♔United States♔United Cerebral Palsy♔♔♔♔♔♔United Cerebral Palsy of Tennessee asks the Department to guarantee that an actual person evaluates every AI-generated insurance denial before accelerating any artificial intelligence adoption in coverage or care.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0013♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Johnson♔♔Kansas♔♔United States♔American Chiropractic Association♔♔♔♔♔♔American Chiropractic Association Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎⏎⏎On behalf of the American Chiropractic Association and its 1,200 member organizations, we are genuinely alarmed by the sequencing embedded in this RFI. HHS is asking how to accelerate adoption before the governance architecture exists to make that safe. When we surveyed our membership in the third quarter of 2024, over two-thirds of respondents reported fielding patient grievances regarding algorithm-guided care decisions. At the same time, under thirty percent currently utilize formal informed-consent procedures for such tools.⏎⏎⏎⏎American Chiropractic Association Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎⏎⏎Once this technology is embedded in clinical workflows, it does not come back out. We watched that with EHR rollouts. The objection here is not to AI. It is to moving permanently before the democratic and regulatory foundations⏎⏎⏎⏎exist to support accountability. HHS should reframe this RFI around precautionary governance, not acceleration. Bias auditing requirements and data privacy disclosures remain largely absent from current frameworks, and safety-net facilities are bearing the consequences disproportionately.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0014♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Miller♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My client Rosa is 81 years old and has been on the same insulin regimen for years, and when her clnic switched to some automated system it flagged her dosage and her doctor spent the whole appointment arguing with a computer screen rather than paying attention to his patient. Two weeks later I drove her to the ER because her blood sugar crashed. I've read about big insurance companies running computer programs that reject thousands of claims at once, no human ever looks at them. Rosa doesn't speak much English and she has no way to fight the decision. The vast majority of folks just abandon the process. The federal health department must demand that medical staff personally review every single rejection. Failing to step in means you are deciding to permit these systemic failures to persist. I want whoever reads this to think about what Rosa would do if I wasn't there to drive her.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0015♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Brown♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔National Rural Health Association♔♔♔♔♔♔The National Rural Health Association urges HHS to require independent evaluations of equity outcomes prior to expanding the rollout of artificial intelligence across safety-net providers that treat marginalized communities.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0016♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Scott♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔Express Scripts♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of Express Scripts, I am submitting these comments in response to the request for information regarding artificial intelligence in healthcare. Our compliance team actively monitors Federal Register activity, and we note that this RFI was published with less visibility than is typical for decisions that will ultimately affect how AI tools are deployed across major payer networks nationwide. ⏎⏎The pressure to accelerate AI adoption in clinical and administrative workflows is significant, and we recognize that our clients experience friction from inconsistent payer standards. However, the existing regulatory frameworks perform necessary oversight that has not been adequately replaced by industry consensus. We have observed measurable disparities in how certain predictive models perform across different patient populations, particularly regarding widely utilized clinical algorithms like sepsis prediction models. Similarly, reviews across the managed care sector have identified demographic variances in automated prior authorization and utilization management tools. These instances demonstrate the compliance risks of deploying tools that outpace standardized validation protocols. ⏎⏎Removing regulatory friction before federal agencies establish clear bias auditing requirements will inevitably lead to these technologies reaching populations they were not adequately trained or tested on. From a corporate compliance and risk management perspective, the liability exposure associated with that scenario has not been sufficiently accounted for in the current cost-benefit analyses we have reviewed regarding healthcare AI integration.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0017♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔Miller♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rcvd via fax 802-555-0147 | 4 pp total⏎Page 1 of 4⏎⏎My name is Anthony Miller. I am a licensed clinical social worker based in Burlington, Vermont, and I have been doing case management work in chronic pain and behavioral health for going on 22 years. I want to address what I think is the most pressing question buried in this RFI: what happens to patients who are already marginalized when we deploy AI tools at scale before we have any reliable way to measure whether those tools are hurting them.⏎⏎I will start with a specific situation because I think it matters more than abstract argument.⏎⏎A client of mine -- I will call him Darnell, 47 years old, Black, Burlington resident -- came to me after being flagged by intake software at two separate clinics as high-risk for substance misuse. This happened before any physician had spoken to him. He was there for chronic back pain. He spent the better part of four months in appointment after appointment having to argue against a characterization the algorithm had already made. I watched him do that. It was exhausting and it was wrong and nobody at either clinic could tell me exactly what data had produced the flag or how to contest it.⏎⏎WHAT WE ALREADY KNOW⏎⏎The research on this is not speculative. Obermeyer and colleagues published work in Science in 2019 showing that a widely used commercial algorithm systematically underestimated illness severity in Black patients -- not because the algorithm was programmed to discriminate, but because it was trained on cost data that already reflected decades of unequal access to care. The bias was baked into the inputs. And then Sjoding et al. in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 documented that pulse oximeters -- a much simpler technology -- were overestimating oxygen saturation in patients with darker skin pigmentation, which meant some patients were not getting supplemental oxygen during COVID when they needed it.⏎⏎Those two findings alone should give HHS serious pause. We are not talking about edge cases.⏎⏎Page 2 of 4⏎⏎MY CONCERN WITH ACCELERATION⏎⏎The RFI asks how HHS can accelerate AI adoption. My concern is that rushing this integration while failing to require strict equity evaluations means we are embedding these known failure modes at scale before anyone has the infrastructure to identify them. Once a tool is deployed across hundreds of clinical sites and reimbursement structures are built around its outputs, removing or correcting it becomes enormously difficult. The harm is not distributed evenly. It lands on patients like Darnell.⏎⏎I want to be specific about what I am asking for:⏎⏎1. HHS should require that any AI clinical decision support tool undergo independent bias auditing -- across race, ethnicity, and income -- before deployment, not as a post-market study.⏎⏎2. Audit results should be publicly available in plain language, not buried in a vendor submission.⏎⏎3. Patients should have a documented right to know when an AI tool contributed to a clinical recommendation about them.⏎⏎I am not opposed to AI in healthcare. I have seen scheduling tools and documentation tools that genuinely help. But Darnell's situation was not unusual, and it constantly worries me to consider the sheer volume of comparable incidents that happen under the radar because the affected person didn't have a social worker to contact.⏎⏎Page 3 of 4⏎⏎The consent form Darnell signed at the second clinic was two pages and referenced the software only as "decision support technology." He asked me afterward what that meant.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0018♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Wilson♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔Medical University of South Carolina♔♔♔♔♔♔The Committee on Emerging Surgical Technology and Education at the Medical University of South Carolina submits the following perspectives regarding this Request for Information concerning the acceleration of clinical AI adoption. A 2024 survey of 1,847 surgeon-researchers found that 68% rated real-world AI outcome literature as insufficient to guide adoption decisions.⏎⏎Fewer than 30% of 112 reviewed AI-assisted surgical tools had been evaluated beyond original developer datasets. HHS should prioritize external validation research and implementation science before accelerating deployment.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0019♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔Taylor♔♔Texas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My crewmate Dale nearly died because an algorithm flagged him low-risk twice. You need to pump the brakes and demand hard proof before unleashing these programs on real patients.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0020♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Martinez♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My client Darlene waited four months while an algorithm kept calling her low priority, and when a human finally overrode it, the clinician said the tool had missed everything that actually mattered.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0021♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Garcia♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔Families USA♔♔♔♔♔♔Families USA submits the following comments regarding Docket No. HHS-OCR-2023-0014 on the regulation of AI-assisted clinical tools.⏎⏎⏎⏎When reviewing recent diagnostic software rollouts at several Minnesota safety-net clinics, our policy team found that medical staff had no access to documentation showing whether these platforms were actually tested on diverse patient populations. (See Families USA Issue Brief, "Digital Health in the Safety Net," Oct. 2023).⏎⏎⏎⏎Current civil rights rules must explicitly cover these new clinical systems. If we incentivize fast implementation without demanding that software creators demonstrate equitable accuracy across diverse racial and ethnic demographics, we are simply hardwiring existing disparities into the code. This inevitably results in faulty diagnoses for underserved communities. HHS must require clear disclosure of software limitations and mandate demographic testing data before these clinical tools can be authorized for use in Medicaid-funded facilities.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0022♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Thompson♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔Redox♔♔♔♔♔♔Redox Comments on HHS Health Sector AI RFI | ONC-HHS-2024 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎⏎Before addressing the substance of this request for information, I want to raise a procedural question that may seem simple: is there a public hearing scheduled for this RFI? Our organization would strongly prefer to offer testimony in person rather than through written submission alone. The compliance and clinical concerns we have accumulated across our client base over the past two years are genuinely difficult to convey in a formal written comment, and I suspect we are not alone in that feeling.⏎⏎⏎⏎That said, we recognize written comment is the mechanism available, and so we offer the following observations.⏎⏎⏎⏎Redox works with health systems representing more than 12 million covered lives. What we have observed in that work is fundamentally disconnected from software engineering challenges in the conventional sense. It is a documentation problem masquerading as a technical hurdle, and a liability problem masquerading as a documentation problem.⏎⏎⏎⏎Across a cross-client compliance audit completed in Q2 2024, we identified an average of 4.3 separate regulatory and paperwork mandates demanded by individual insurers per AI tool submission. The cost burden this generates — roughly $180,000 per product annually — is not the part that concerns us most. What concerns us most is what that compliance overhead actually represents: nobody has decided upon the ultimate bearer of liability when an algorithmic clinical recommendation proves incorrect.⏎⏎⏎⏎Redox Comments on HHS Health Sector AI RFI | ONC-HHS-2024 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎⏎In 91% of payer contracts we reviewed during that same period, liability attribution for AI recommendations remained unresolved. That is not a drafting oversight. That is a structural ambiguity that the acceleration frame this RFI uses does not adequately address.⏎⏎⏎⏎The research on the therapeutic alliance between medical professionals and those they treat is relevant here. Kelley et al. (2014, PLOS ONE 9(4): e94207) documented through meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials that the relational dimension of care produces measurable outcome effects independent of the clinical intervention itself. An AI tool that interposes itself into that dynamic without clear accountability structures does not merely create legal ambiguity — it potentially degrades something with documented therapeutic value.⏎⏎⏎⏎We are not arguing against AI utilization. We work every day to make AI interoperability function. But the governing question this RFI poses — how to speed up industry integration — is the wrong starting question. The question HHS should be asking is whether the accountability infrastructure exists to make deployment safe, and if not, what the Department intends to do about it before the pace of implementation outruns the capacity for redress.⏎⏎⏎⏎The 68% of AI tools we identified as falling into a regulatory gray zone under current FDA and ONC frameworks (see 88 Fed. Reg. 23746) suggests that infrastructure does not yet♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0023♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Anderson♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔City of Hope National Medical Center♔♔♔♔♔♔City of Hope National Medical Center welcomes this opportunity to respond to HHS's request for public input on AI adoption in clinical care. Algorithmic prior authorization systems have demonstrably increased denial rates for medically necessary care among elderly, disabled, and financially vulnerable individuals.⏎⏎HHS possesses existing jurisdiction to mandate direct personnel oversight whenever algorithms govern insurance approval decisions. Accelerating adoption without exercising this power is a foreseeable policy failure with identifiable victims.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0024♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Hill♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔American Health Information Management Association♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the American Health Information Management Association, HHS must require equity impact assessments and plain-language disclosure before any AI tool reaches patients in Medicaid-serving facilities.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0025♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Wright♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist in Fairbanks retired two winters back and it took nearly eight months to find a replacement, and during that time I had a remote monitoring alert flag something that turned out to be a false reading -- they almost flew me to Anchorage over nothing. I'm not against the technology, it helped me get through those eight months, but what worries me is that hospitals will look at AI and see a reason to cut the schedulers and the billing staff and the people who actually catch the machine's mistakes, and those are mostly women, a lot of them women of color, doing work that doesn't get noticed until it's gone. Don't be in such a huge rush to roll this out. Make absolutely certain that flesh-and-blood workers with the power to make the final call are overseeing things before pushing this wide in places like mine.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0026♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Anderson♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔Consumers Union♔♔♔♔♔♔Consumers Union expects HHS to mandate enforceable human accountability measures before speeding up artificial intelligence integration in clinical settings, as the therapeutic trust between patients and providers is irreplaceable.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0027♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Hall♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network♔♔♔♔♔♔April 14, 2025⏎⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎United States Health and Human Services Department⏎⏎The American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network respectfully requests that HHS require all AI-generated coverage denials to be evaluated by real medical staff before the adoption of these tools accelerates any further.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Ashley Hall⏎Campaign Director⏎American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0028♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Garcia♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔American Heart Association♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Heart Association formally requests that HHS implement compulsory equity impact assessments prior to accelerating AI adoption within clinical environments that treat at-risk communities.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0029♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Smith♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife Margaret drove eighty miles round-trip in 2019 just to see a cardiologist in Morgantown. I certainly get why folks are eager for quicker medical results. But somebody ought to ask who exactly pockets the savings when hospitals replace billing staff with algorithms.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0030♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Anderson♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To the Office of the Deputy Secretary, U.S. Department of Health and Human Services:⏎⏎⏎⏎I taught high school history for thirty-two years before retiring, and I always told my students to read the fine print before they agree to anything. I wish my wife Carol's doctors had done the same. She was sick for almost a year while her clinic's software kept pushing an anxiety diagnosis. Her kidney function was just getting destroyed by an autoimmune disease the whole time. We finally went to a solo rheumatologist up in Fort Collins who actually figured it out, mostly because he just ignored the computer screen and listened to Carol talk for forty-five minutes. We lost so much time. Seeing this document about the government trying to fast-track artificial intelligence in medicine just made my stomach sink.⏎⏎⏎⏎I honestly cannot understand the desperate hurry to roll this out. You're asking about barriers to getting this tech into hospitals but leaving out the biggest issue. Who is actually at fault when an AI messes up and hurts somebody? Right now the answer seems to be nobody. When you try to hold someone accountable it's just a giant shell game. The doctor says it's the software's fault, but then the software company has some sneaky clause in their contract saying they don't make medical decisions. And the hospital will just say well the FDA cleared it so we're safe. So the patient who actually got hurt is left holding the bag with zero way to get justice.⏎⏎⏎⏎After Carol got her diagnosis I spent months digging into what went wrong. It took me three solid months of records requests to get a copy of the hospital's contract with that software vendor. When I finally read it, I couldn't believe my eyes. The tech company actually wrote into the contract that they aren't responsible for diagnostic mistakes. They are selling a medical tool but taking zero responsibility if it breaks. It made my blood boil. It still does.⏎⏎⏎⏎And now HHS wants to shove more of these systems into clinics without fixing the liability rules first? That's completely backwards. Before you encourage hospitals to buy more of this stuff, you need clear rules establishing exactly who is held liable if the algorithm makes an error. The FDA shouldn't be approving anything until that is ironed out. You also need to stop letting these tech companies write contracts that let them off the hook for bad outcomes. If they sell a product to make medical decisions, they need to stand behind it. Someone also needs to look into how hospitals and tech companies are using FDA approval as a shield to avoid malpractice lawsuits.⏎⏎⏎⏎I'm not a lawyer, just an old history teacher. But reading your document, it's pretty obvious your agency just wants to move fast and is fishing for excuses to do exactly that. Figuring out responsibility when AI messes up isn't something you can just sort out down the road after the tools are already everywhere. It has to be step one. Otherwise it's just plain wrong to use them on people. Slow down and fix the accountability problem before someone else's wife loses a year of her life to a computer glitch.⏎⏎⏎⏎Mark Anderson⏎⏎⏎⏎Loveland, Colorado♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0031♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Robinson♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔National Federation of the Blind♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the National Federation of the Blind, HHS should require open interoperability standards before any federal credibility or data access flows to AI clinical vendors, or this agency will repeat the EHR consolidation mistake at a scale no one can undo.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0032♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah♔Gonzalez♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Two winters ago my crew pulled a man out of a burning warehouse on Gratiot Avenue, and by the time paramedics got him stable, an automated triage system had already flagged him as lower priority. The attending had to manually override it. Nobody could explain why the system scored him that way. That incident has really stuck with me, because I've since learned this isn't rare. We already know about blood oxygen monitors giving false readings for patients of color, early warning programs for blood infections missing sick individuals, and insurance algorithms automatically denying care at much higher rates for marginalized neighborhoods. And HHS wants to accelerate adoption. I'm not saying AI has no place in medicine, but it makes zero sense to me that speed is the priority when we can't yet prove these tools work equitably for everyone, not just on average across a population.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0033♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel♔Baker♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔1upHealth♔♔♔♔♔♔1upHealth Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 1⏎⏎Submitted to: U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎⏎Re: Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Submitted by: Daniel Baker, Director of Health Information Technology, 1upHealth⏎⏎Bismarck, ND⏎⏎---⏎⏎PRELIMINARY CONCERN: THE EHR PRECEDENT HHS APPEARS TO HAVE FORGOTTEN⏎⏎Fifteen years of HITECH implementation should have taught HHS something about what happens when federal adoption incentives are deployed without structural market safeguards. Academic reviews from 2017 published in Health Affairs highlighted how rapid EHR adoption, federally subsidized and poorly conditioned, produced a market dominated by just a handful of incumbent vendors whose proprietary data architectures made switching functionally impossible for most health systems. We are now watching HHS position itself to repeat that mistake, at greater scale, in a domain where the clinical stakes are considerably higher.⏎⏎The push for rapid deployment evident throughout this request for information concerns me for reasons that are not theoretical.⏎⏎---⏎⏎REGARDING MARKET STRUCTURE AND VENDOR LOCK-IN⏎⏎A 2024 benchmarking analysis conducted across 1,200 affiliated health systems by PINC AI indicated over two-thirds of surveyed participants identified inconsistent payer-side evaluation criteria as the primary barrier to scaling AI-enabled clinical decision support tools. Our own Q3 2024 policy working group, drawn from 47 member organizations, documented a median of 14 distinct payer documentation requirements for the same class of non-device AI tool — generating estimated compliance overhead of $340,000 annually per mid-sized health system. That is not a market functioning competitively. That is a market already fragmenting in ways that favor incumbents who can absorb compliance costs.⏎⏎The structural dynamics driving this fragmentation are not difficult to identify:⏎⏎1. Proprietary API layers controlled by dominant EHR vendors create integration dependencies that function as switching costs regardless of AI tool performance.⏎⏎2. Vendors participating in HHS public-private research partnerships gain privileged access to federal data pipelines and regulatory development cycles — a form of regulatory capture that disadvantages smaller competitors before the market matures.⏎⏎3. Once AI systems are embedded in billing, prior authorization, and clinical documentation workflows, health systems discover that ripping out these tools invariably costs more than simply tolerating their inherent flaws. That is not a hypothetical. Dafny, Gruber, and Ody (2023) documented precisely this dynamic in adjacent health technology consolidation contexts.⏎⏎1upHealth Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 2⏎⏎The ONC's own 2020 policy brief on health IT market competition documented anticompetitive effects from incumbent EHR vendor integration requirements under 21st Century Cures Act implementation — and CFR 45 Part 170 certification requirements have not resolved those dynamics. Adding an AI layer on top of an already-captured infrastructure layer does not accelerate innovation. It accelerates consolidation.⏎⏎HHS must condition any AI adoption incentive — reimbursement pathway modernization, research partnership participation, any of it — on open standards compliance, data portability requirements enforceable at the contract level, and structural separation between vendors who participate in federal AI research partnerships and those competing⏎⏎dbaker@1uphealth.io♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0034♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Robinson♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔American Academy of Ophthalmology♔♔♔♔♔♔American Academy of Ophthalmology⏎⏎655 Beach Street⏎⏎San Francisco, CA 94109⏎⏎⏎⏎Joseph Robinson⏎⏎Director of Government Affairs⏎⏎⏎⏎June 2025⏎⏎⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎⏎U.S. Dept. of Health and Human Services⏎⏎Hubert H. Humphrey Building⏎⏎Washington, District of Columbia 20201⏎⏎⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎⏎Our national medical association of eye physicians and surgeons welcomes the chance to provide our perspective on this Request for Information. We are genuinely encouraged by HHS's interest in expanding AI's role in clinical care, and our members have seen real promise in AI-assisted imaging for conditions like diabetic retinopathy.⏎⏎⏎⏎What we want to raise, though, is something our members describe with some regularity. A patient arrives, perhaps with a family member translating, clearly frightened, unable to fully articulate what is wrong. The ophthalmologist reads something in that exchange that no diagnostic system would capture.⏎⏎⏎⏎AAO Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎⏎⏎When AI mediates that encounter, even a technically sound recommendation can displace the relational presence that makes the recommendation meaningful. The patient stops being a person someone is responsible for.⏎⏎⏎⏎We are not arguing against adoption. We are asking HHS to resist the assumption that faster deployment is better deployment. Our members in rural Alabama and elsewhere tell us that individuals residing in resource-strained regions are already skeptical of systems they feel do not see them fully. Adding an algorithmic layer without requiring meaningful human accountability at the point of care risks deepening that skepticism considerably.⏎⏎⏎⏎HHS should establish clear expectations that AI tools in clinical settings operate in support of the interpersonal dynamic between provider and patient, not as a substitute for it.⏎⏎⏎⏎The Academy welcomes further conversation with HHS on what human-centered standards for AI deployment might look like in practice.⏎⏎⏎⏎jrobinson@aao.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0035♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Anderson♔♔Kentucky♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My granddaughter Leah got misdiagnosed twice at our local clinic before we drove to a specialist in Lexington. The doctor only figured out she had an immune disease because he was actually sitting there looking at her, and he saw her hands shaking while she talked about her stomach. A computer program isnt going to see that it just looks at numbers.⏎⏎⏎⏎Out here in rural Kentucky our clinics are stretched so thin already, I worry these places without much money will just use AI to get rid of nurses to save a buck. We struggle enough to see a real doctor as it is. You have got to establish some strict guidelines to hit the brakes on this, folks out here cant fight back and we shouldn't be the test cases just because we live out in the country.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0036♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔King♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔National Immigration Law Center♔♔♔♔♔♔I write for the National Immigration Law Center. HHS must require human oversight of AI coverage denials before expanding adoption further.⏎⏎Non-English speakers cannot navigate appeals alone. As I see locally in Arkansas, rural Medicaid enrollees face similar barriers.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0037♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Davis♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔American Stroke Association♔♔♔♔♔♔Dear HHS Officials:⏎⏎⏎⏎We must determine who is at fault before bringing these algorithms into medical practice. Otherwise, injured patients are left stranded when the software creator and the hospital simply blame each other.⏎⏎⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Joseph Davis⏎⏎American Stroke Association♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0038♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Jones♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔American Psychiatric Association♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Psychiatric Association formally asks the Department to suspend the rapid adoption of clinical AI until required protocols for assessing algorithmic prejudice, disaggregated performance reporting by race and income, and independent post-deployment monitoring are established.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0039♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔Clark♔♔Wyoming♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter spent eight months being rushed through appointments until a doctor finally figured out she had an autoimmune disease. What worries me about AI adoption is that hospitals will use it to cut staff faster, not to slow down and look harder. The schedulers and billing clerks who actually knew Kayla's file were women. Most were women of color. They are the ones who lose jobs when the algorithm arrives.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0040♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Wright♔♔Virginia♔♔United States♔Hologic♔♔♔♔♔♔RE: Docket No. HHS-ONC-2024-0012 ⏎⏎⏎⏎On behalf of Hologic, a medical device and diagnostics manufacturer, I submit these comments as Chief Medical Officer regarding the proposed acceleration of clinical AI. We are concerned HHS is pushing to accelerate AI adoption before establishing a practical, vendor-neutral governance framework. While deploying our diagnostic imaging systems, we observe our hospital customers struggling with numerous third-party AI solutions that lack basic liability documentation. Additionally, our provider partners report that inconsistent payer standards for AI-assisted diagnostics cause substantial reimbursement delays. We watched this sector struggle through the painful process of EHR consolidation over the last decade. We worry HHS underestimates how difficult it will be to untangle proprietary AI integrations once they are federally subsidized into permanence.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0041♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Perez♔♔California♔♔United States♔Leukemia and Lymphoma Society♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the Leukemia and Lymphoma Society, we want to be direct: accelerating AI adoption before accountability structures exist guarantees that harmed patients will have no recourse. When something goes wrong, the clinician, hospital, and vendor each point elsewhere.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0042♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Lee♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I saw this HHS rule about AI tools in clinical settings and wanted to comment because it makes me really nervous about where things are heading with doctors.⏎In February, our little girl Mia got flagged at a school health screening for a potential heart issue, and we basically panicked for three weeks waiting to see the specialist. When we finally got to Nemours Children's Health in Wilmington the pediatric cardiologist there was so great, she just talked to us like normal people and explained Mia probably had this murmur since birth and it was totally harmless. It felt incredibly reassuring to look her in the eye and know we were dealing with an actual physician with genuine expertise, rather than someone just reading off a monitor.⏎If a computer algorithm had been the one to deliver that initial warning to us, I probably would have completely lost my mind. Think about a machine just spitting out a diagnosis without a real doctor sitting there to explain why you shouldn't panic. HHS needs to make absolutely sure that a human presence is always mandatory when using these AI tools in medicine. We can't just rely on computer outputs for stuff like this, you have to have a real person there to explain it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0043♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Davis♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔Ohio State University College of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔The Ohio State University College of Medicine formally requests that HHS mandate validated, site-specific performance benchmarks prior to approving or reimbursing any clinical AI deployment under federal programs.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0044♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Robinson♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My granddaughter Tanya sat me down at my kitchen table in Hattiesburg and showed me some news stories about a big insurance company up north that was using artificial intelligence to automatically reject medical requests, thousands of them, and the people being turned down were elderly or sick and most of them just gave up. I had been feeling good about the AI my doctor uses on my mammograms but this is different. HHS must make it mandatory for an actual person to review any automated rejection before it takes effect.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0045♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔King♔♔Ohio♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To be completely honest, I have a hard time knowing if this relates to the major national health care overhaul from the Obama era or a totally separate initiative, since I keep reading these updates and getting them all mixed up. What I do know is that two years ago some software flagged my heart problem as low priority before any doctor had looked at my file, and I sat with that for three weeks while the real issue went unnoticed. I've also heard that insurance companies use similar software to deny claims by the thousands without a physician ever reviewing them, and most people never appeal because they have no idea how. Please don't speed any of this up until there's a human being who is actually responsible when something gets denied wrong.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0046♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Moore♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Forty-one years at Ruby Memorial, and the one case that always stands out in my mind is a man I will call Mr. Elkins, 68 years old, Black, from Morgantown. His sepsis presentation came in flagged low-risk by the algorithm because his baseline vitals ran chronically elevated, a pattern the system had no context to interpret. A nurse caught it. He went to the ICU instead of home. That was not a near-miss caused by a bad algorithm in the abstract. It was a foreseeable consequence of deploying a tool without asking first whether it had been validated on patients who look like Mr. Elkins.⏎⏎I want to raise something before anything else, which is whether HHS is holding any public hearings on this request for information, because I would much rather say this in person than try to get it right in writing. I am a nurse, not a policy attorney.⏎⏎WHAT CONCERNS ME ABOUT THE FRAMING⏎⏎The way this RFI is written, acceleration is treated as the goal, and equity considerations are positioned somewhere downstream, as things to address after adoption is already underway. I think that gets the sequence exactly wrong.⏎⏎HHS already has nondiscrimination obligations that apply here. The Office for Civil Rights has issued guidance on Section 1557 of the federal healthcare statute and how it applies to algorithmic decision-making tools in health care settings. That guidance exists. The question is whether this department intends to enforce it as a precondition for any reimbursement incentives tied to AI, or whether it will be treated as a checkbox after the fact.⏎⏎Choosing to accelerate adoption without equity guardrails already in place is not a neutral act. It is an affirmative policy choice with foreseeable disparate impact on the patients least likely to benefit and most likely to be harmed.⏎⏎WHAT I AM ASKING FOR⏎⏎Before HHS ties any reimbursement incentive to AI adoption, I am asking that the department require:⏎⏎1. An equity impact assessment conducted before deployment, not after, covering the specific patient populations at the facility where the tool will be used.⏎⏎2. Independent clinical oversight, meaning someone with no financial relationship to the vendor, reviewing that assessment and signing off on it.⏎⏎3. A clear mechanism for frontline staff, nurses included, to flag concerns about algorithmic outputs without fear of being dismissed as resistant to technology.⏎⏎That third one matters more than it probably sounds. At Ruby Memorial I have watched nurses catch things the machines missed. That is not anecdote, that is the actual workflow. I cannot get behind any regulation that bypasses or diminishes that essential human intuition simply for the sake of pushing patients through the system faster.⏎⏎I genuinely do not know if there is a comment period extension available or whether there is a contact at ONC I can reach directly, because I have more to say about the rural West Virginia context specifically and♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0047♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Flores♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔American Medical Association♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Medical Association advises HHS to require enforceable labor impact assessments as a prerequisite for any artificial intelligence acceleration initiatives. Deploying AI without worker and patient standing to contest automated decisions transfers accountability to vendors with no obligation to either.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0048♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Lee♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔American Academy of Hospice and Palliative Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Representing hospice and palliative care professionals, AAHPM requests that HHS pause its acceleration efforts, commission independent public deliberation, and condition any reimbursement action on governance frameworks built before AI embeds irreversibly in clinical care.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0049♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Wilson♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔Providence Health & Services♔♔♔♔♔♔Comment of Andrew Wilson, CFO, Providence Health & Services⏎⏎⏎⏎Submitted in response to HHS Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎⏎The framing of this RFI is problematic. "Accelerating adoption" is a promotional objective, not a regulatory one. Having managed technology budgets through the HITECH Act era and watched what Meaningful Use incentives actually produced, I am not prepared to endorse the premise that speed is the right policy variable to optimize here. We did not get a competitive market of interoperable EHR vendors back then; we got a consolidated oligopoly with extraction pricing and switching costs that now run into the tens of millions per migration. Prominent health informatics researchers have documented this exact dynamic: federal funding aimed at forcing rapid implementation successfully elevated adoption metrics but entirely bypassed the underlying market structure, ultimately yielding widespread vendor lock-in.⏎⏎⏎⏎The reimbursement question HHS poses cannot be answered honestly without first asking who benefits from the architecture being designed. In recent benchmarking discussions with peer health system CFOs, payer inconsistency in AI reimbursement coding is routinely identified as the primary deployment barrier. The per-site administrative overhead for managing payer-specific compliance frameworks easily runs into the hundreds of thousands of dollars annually. That overhead is not random. It accrues disproportionately to health systems without the contracting leverage to standardize terms, creating a cost structure that only large integrated vendors can absorb efficiently.⏎⏎⏎⏎We can already recognize the structural conditions assembling in AI vendor markets. Developers are relying on proprietary EHR integration architectures that create severe switching costs before any performance deficiency becomes actionable. Public-private research partnerships are giving incumbent vendors privileged access to federal data and regulatory processes. Reimbursement coding frameworks are being designed without mandatory interoperability requirements, and liability language remains completely unresolved across numerous indemnification scenarios between developers, deploying institutions, and attending providers.⏎⏎⏎⏎After an AI clinical decision support tool becomes entrenched in daily physician workflows, ripping the software out is almost always far more expensive than simply putting up with its flaws. Obermeyer et al. (2019) demonstrated that embedded algorithmic systems produce systemic harms institutions cannot easily remediate after lock-in occurs. That is not a theoretical concern.⏎⏎⏎⏎HHS should commission an independent market structure review of AI vendor concentration before finalizing any reimbursement pathway. Mandatory open interoperability standards with enforcement teeth, not voluntary frameworks, must precede any federal reimbursement expansion. Furthermore, HHS should prohibit exclusive data-sharing arrangements between federal agencies and commercial AI vendors in any future pilot programs or regulatory sandboxes.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0050♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Martinez♔♔Kentucky♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔SECTION I. THE HARM IS HAPPENING NOW⏎⏎My daughter Renee spent eleven months being told it was probably stress before a doctor in Lexington finally diagnosed her with lupus. I saw an article about how UnitedHealth let a computer program reject rehab coverage for older folks so often that the medical staff warned it was unsafe. Those are not separate problems. The health department has the power to demand that a real human being looks over and signs off on every single denial. Speed is not the goal. Accountability is.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0051♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Jackson♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔Future of Privacy Forum♔♔♔♔♔♔The Future of Privacy Forum strongly recommends that HHS mandate independent workforce impact analysis and enforceable worker protections before accelerating AI adoption in any clinical setting.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0052♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Lee♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔Cigna♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa Lee, Practice Manager⏎⏎⏎⏎Cigna Care Delivery⏎⏎⏎⏎Des Moines, Iowa⏎⏎⏎⏎Submitted via Regulations.gov⏎⏎⏎⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information on Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎⏎Working on the clinical delivery side of Cigna here in Des Moines, our practice has been actively integrating AI to help with clinical documentation. We want to see a federal framework that supports this kind of innovation. That said, looking through this RFI, I noticed a major blind spot regarding how automated systems are currently being weaponized by external Medicare Advantage plans for prior authorizations.⏎⏎⏎⏎Because we operate as a provider clinic, my front desk team deals with dozens of different MA plans and secondary insurers every week. Throughout recent quarters, my referral coordination team has been overwhelmed by a massive spike in prior authorization rejections for standard post-acute care transfers, particularly concerning our elderly population requiring skilled nursing placement within Polk County. This isn't because our doctors changed how they practice. It's because some of these external plans have rolled out predictive algorithms that just auto-deny claims. I know the recent Senate investigations called out several insurers for doing exactly this with clinically indefensible denial rates, and we are feeling the operational brunt of it on the ground.⏎⏎⏎⏎It is incredibly frustrating to see data from the OIG showing that a massive chunk of these MA denials don't even align with Medicare coverage criteria. We know from experience that almost none of our elderly or low-income patients have the bandwidth to navigate a complex appeal process. When an algorithm kicks back a denial for a non-English speaking patient needing a skilled nursing transition, that patient usually just gives up. You don't need academic studies to see that these automated systems inherently disadvantage people who don't have a dedicated care navigator to fight for them.⏎⏎⏎⏎HHS shouldn't be encouraging faster AI adoption without putting some guardrails on how insurance companies use it to restrict care. CMS already has the authority to regulate MA plans here. At a minimum, there has to be actual human review by a qualified medical professional before an algorithm can deny a claim. Providers and patients also need to see the actual logic behind a denial. Right now, we just get a generic code that tells us nothing, which drags out the appeal timeline until it's too late to help the patient.⏎⏎⏎⏎Physician groups have been sounding the alarm about care delays tied to prior authorizations for a while now. My staff spends hours on the phone every single day just trying to get a human medical director on the line to override an obvious algorithmic error. The administrative waste is staggering, but watching our patients suffer adverse events because their approval was stuck in an automated queue is what actually needs to be addressed by this framework.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0053♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Sanchez♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔We received two incorrect diagnoses for my little girl Mia until a physician in Portland finally took the proper time to figure out the real problem. I think about that every time I hear a hospital system announce that a new technology will reduce burden and streamline care. In my experience as a teacher, that kind of announcement usually means fewer adults doing more work while the people at the top decide the savings paid for itself. HHS should require hospitals to demonstrate, before these tools are approved for wider use, that patient outcomes actually improved and that frontline staff counts did not quietly shrink as a result.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0054♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Johnson♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔AARP♔♔♔♔♔♔AARP Georgia is submitting this comment regarding the HHS request for input on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care. During our recent state-level listening sessions, older adults repeatedly pointed to the lack of transparency in healthcare algorithms as a major barrier to navigating their health. Patients almost never know when a computer is making a critical coverage decision, and we are already seeing these automated systems frequently deny necessary care for our members.⏎⏎HHS should not accelerate a process that is already producing measurable harm in the real world. Prior to approving faster adoption, clearance must be firmly conditioned upon developers providing performance data broken down by demographic factors (including age, race, and sex). No acceleration should proceed unless developers are required to conduct bias audits prior to deployment in clinical settings. Additionally, patients must be explicitly informed whenever AI plays a role in their diagnoses or coverage determinations.⏎⏎We know exactly what happens when medical technology is deployed without adequate safeguards in place. The clinical failure of pulse oximeters to accurately read oxygen levels in darker-skinned patients went completely unaddressed for decades. Rushing AI integration sets up older adults for similar systemic failures if we fail to mandate these audits and transparency requirements today.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0055♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Perez♔♔Ohio♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My name is Patricia Perez, I'm a nurse in Ohio with over thirty years on the floor, and I honestly cannot figure out if this AI regulation is tied to Obamacare or if it is a completely different program altogether. I see all sorts of agency names being thrown around and it's hard to follow, but my main point is that before we rush into anything, the regulations must guarantee that a living, breathing person evaluates every single care denial, because I have personally witnessed the terrible fallout when a computer system misjudges a sick patient.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0056♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Brown♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife's thyroid cancer was nearly missed because an automated screening system decided her threat level was minimal and sent her home. That was at a clinic in Duluth, and it took three more weeks and a second physician to catch it. I hope the agency realizes that figuring out the extent of AI involvement in healthcare isn't simply a programming issue for bureaucrats and tech vendors to dictate to the rest of us. It's a moral question, a democratic one, and the public hasn't really been asked. Once these systems get wired into how hospitals bill and diagnose, no future administration pulls them out, because the switching costs make that practically impossible. Expecting medical care to remain in the hands of accountable humans is a perfectly reasonable moral position. Slow down, build the oversight first, and let the average citizen have a real voice on the matter prior to making this permanent.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0057♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Taylor♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔National Partnership for Women and Families♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the National Partnership for Women and Families, our organization recommends that the Department require comprehensive evaluations of worker outcomes prior to broadening the deployment of AI. The personnel displaced by these automated tools heavily skew toward female employees and minority populations.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0058♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Hall♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔National Alliance for Caregiving♔♔♔♔♔♔National Alliance for Caregiving⏎James Hall, Community Organizer⏎Sioux Falls, South Dakota 57101⏎⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎The National Alliance for Caregiving is concerned that HHS is preparing to accelerate AI adoption before addressing a documented pattern of harm. In our own 2023 member survey, Black and Hispanic MS patients reported AI-related care delays at nearly twice the rate of white respondents. The UnitedHealth/NaviHealth situation is not an isolated case.⏎⏎Most people who receive an AI-generated denial never appeal. Not because the denial was correct, but because the appeals process is designed to be exhausting. The department already holds the regulatory authority to require actual human beings to comprehensively review these algorithmic coverage decisions, but HHS continues to choose inaction.⏎⏎That choice has consequences our members in South Dakota are already living with.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0059♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Jones♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist here in Tucson started using an AI program on my EKGs about eighteen months back and it caught something she said she might have waited on otherwise. So I'm not against this technology. But the thing that really bothers me is that insurers are already relying on algorithms to reject coverage at enormous scale, and I read about what UnitedHealth was doing with that NaviHealth system cutting off post-acute rehab for older folks who had no idea they could even dispute it. A lot of folks just throw in the towel. A neighbor on fixed income, a veteran who can't pay a lawyer, they don't fight back. Your department already has the power to mandate that a real person stays accountable for every coverage denial before you push AI adoption any faster, and choosing not to do that is a decision with predictable victims.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0060♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Thompson♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My name is Nancy Thompson and I live in Connecticut. I'm writing this because of what happened to me two years ago when my regular rheumatologist retired. The new doctor I saw spent almost the entire appointment staring at a computer screen typing in my symptoms instead of actually looking at me. She changed my medication based on whatever that dashboard told her to do. I ended up violently ill for a month and a half.⏎⏎I had to go find another specialist just to figure out what went wrong. She told me the software they used probably didn't account for someone my age. I am 75. It makes me so angry to think about. I taught middle school in this state for forty years. If one of my students handed in a paper without checking their sources, they got a failing grade. But somehow medical software companies don't have to verify if their programs actually work on senior citizens before using them on us.⏎⏎You can't let hospitals just buy these programs and rush them into the exam room. I know HHS is supposed to enforce civil rights rules to stop discrimination in medicine. Well, this is a form of discrimination. If the software only works on younger people, that's a huge problem. You must compel these tech businesses to show that their software won't harm any demographic.⏎⏎The most fragile individuals inevitably suffer the worst consequences of these lazy workarounds. Obviously seniors are at risk, but think about immigrants who might struggle to explain their symptoms to a doctor who is just checking boxes on a screen. Or someone with multiple overlapping health conditions. A computer program can't handle all that nuance. I worry sick about the underfunded clinics in poorer neighborhoods. They are already short-staffed. What's stopping them from just letting the computer do all the diagnosing to save money instead of hiring actual doctors?⏎⏎Before any federal funding goes to clinics using this tech, the developers need to show their work. They need to test it on elderly patients. They need to test it on minorities and people from different economic backgrounds. We need to see that public data. And please don't let them just give you a passing overall average score. An average score is completely meaningless if the program gets it right for thirty-year-olds but ends up poisoning someone my age.⏎⏎Furthermore, when a computer screws up, I want to know exactly which person is held accountable. When I got sick, I didn't even know where to start to complain or get it fixed. Patients shouldn't need a law degree or have to hire an attorney to find out why their treatment went wrong. There needs to be a very simple, direct process for people to report these errors and get answers.⏎⏎Federal regulators need to step in and halt this dangerous rush to force unproven technology into doctors' offices. Make them list exactly who they tested the software on. You have to put patient safety first.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0061♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔Sanchez♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔Sonic Healthcare USA♔♔♔♔♔♔Sonic Healthcare USA submits these comments drawing on our 2023 internal review of AI-assisted tools deployed across our clinic network in New Mexico. That review found 64% of our technology vendors flagging reimbursement classification inconsistencies as a primary barrier, with several tools remaining in billing gray zones for more than 14 months after go-live. Our operational team recorded upwards of 40 unique sets of evidentiary rules mandated by individual payers regarding comparable AI decision-support functions. We watched the same dynamic play out during EHR consolidation, and we are watching it again now: once a system is embedded in billing workflows and clinical documentation, the switching cost exceeds the cost of tolerating known problems, and competitive pressure to improve effectively disappears.⏎⏎⏎⏎What concerns our organization most is that the RFI's accelerationist framing risks giving large vendors privileged access to federal data and policy processes that smaller alternatives simply cannot match. HHS should impose interoperability mandates and open data standards before that window closes, not after the infrastructure layer is already captured by a highly consolidated group of vendors whose contracts our network cannot practically exit.⏎⏎⏎⏎Sonic Healthcare USA Comments | HHS-AI-RFI-2024 | Page 2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0062♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔Taylor♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Forty-one years on med floors in Denver, and i've seen every single wave of health tech roll in faster than anyone had the sense to manage it. My sister, two years into a new risk-scoring system at her hospital in Pueblo, got misclassified and almost never got the cardiology referral that found her blockage. her own doctor had to physically override the system. thats not a glitch, thats what happens when the people running these tools cant explain what theyre doing. what really scares me is that once this artificial intelligence gets permanently tangled up in how hospitals bill for care and how floor staff do their charting, it is stuck there for good. no major health IT system ever has been removed. that means this decision forecloses choices the public hasnt even been asked to make yet. slowing down to build real oversight first isnt being anti-technology. its a legitimate choice and HHS shouldnt be taking it off the table before anyone has had a real conversation about what kind of medicine we actually want.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0063♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Clark♔♔Virginia♔♔United States♔Center for Democracy and Technology♔♔♔♔♔♔The Center for Democracy and Technology appreciates the opportunity to respond to this RFI. CDT's work with under-resourced clinic networks has made plain that the existing accountability framework for AI-assisted clinical decisions is not equipped to handle multi-party harm scenarios, where vendors, providers, and payers each hold partial responsibility but none holds clear liability.⏎⏎⏎⏎FDA clearance of an AI tool does not guarantee that the tool performs safely in a specific deployment environment. When these algorithmic tools become deeply integrated into everyday healthcare operations and payment models, pulling them out becomes a financial impossibility even if safety issues are documented.⏎⏎⏎⏎HHS should not accelerate adoption until vendor disclaimer practices are regulated and patients have an identifiable party to hold responsible.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0064♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Paul♔Campbell♔♔Kansas♔♔United States♔Apple Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔Apple Inc. Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎Apple Inc. submits these remarks to address the agency's request for information. Our cross-sector work with health technology organizations gives us a vantage point that we feel obligated to bring to this proceeding. We work with dozens of clinical AI developers and health system deployers, and the pattern we see most consistently is not a regulatory bottleneck — it is a performance gap between what AI tools demonstrate in controlled validation and what they actually do in production environments. This gap is not incidental. It is structural, and the industry's own history tells us so.⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎Sendak et al. (2020) documented what practitioners have long observed: model performance in controlled validation environments routinely overstates real-world utility, sometimes dramatically. This is not a solvable edge case. Real clinical records are inconsistently coded, fragmented across systems, and updated in ways that can render a model's inputs misleading within hours of a clinical encounter. Training data never assumes this level of noise, and most validation protocols do not test for it either.⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎Apple Inc. Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎What concerns us more, though, is the second-order effect. Goddard et al. (2012) documented automation bias systematically across clinical decision support contexts: the better a tool performs in demos and early deployment, the more clinicians calibrate their independent vigilance downward. This means errors concentrate precisely at the moments of system failure, which is exactly backwards from what safety architecture should produce. We saw something like this play out with sepsis prediction algorithms at major health systems — tools that performed well in retrospective validation but generated enough false positives in production that clinicians began dismissing alerts categorically, including alerts that were correct.⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎Obermeyer and Emanuel (2016) argued that health AI overpromising follows a recognizable and recurring pattern, and the EHR rollout disruptions documented by Ash et al. (2004) established that implementation problems in health IT are systemic rather than idiosyncratic. We are not in new territory here. We are in familiar territory with a new technology.⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎An internal assessment we concluded in May 2024 revealed that 68% of health technology clients consider insurer inconsistency to be their biggest hurdle to scaling, as these organizations must navigate an average of 14 separate sets of coverage evidence mandated by individual insurance companies when dealing with equivalent product categories (Apple Inc., 2024). Compliance preparation costs averaged $340,000 per payer contract cycle. These are real frictions, and we understand why accelerating reimbursement pathways is appealing.⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎But reimbursement pathway modernization without prospective, multi-site, population-stratified real-world validation requirements is precisely the kind of acceleration that produces the liability exposure and reputational damage that stall adoption more severely than deliberate regulation would. HHS should fund that validation infrastructure first — not as a condition that delays innovation, but because without it, the⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎Apple Inc. Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 3⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎payer friction problem does not actually resolve.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0065♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Rivera♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔Komodo Health♔♔♔♔♔♔RE: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Submitted by: Thomas Rivera, Practice Manager, Komodo Health, Washington State⏎⏎Date: March 2025⏎⏎⏎⏎Looking back at the HITECH Act of 2009 provides a clear warning. The federal subsidies meant to accelerate EHR adoption across hospitals largely resulted in market consolidation rather than clinical transformation. As researchers like Adler-Milstein and Jha pointed out in Health Affairs, the gains from HITECH mostly went to a few dominant vendors. This created integration issues that locked health systems into long-term contracts, making it nearly impossible to switch vendors without causing major workflow disruptions. It looks like HHS is preparing to follow a very similar path with AI. In our practice operations, our contracts team is seeing this exact dynamic playing out in real time across dozens of client relationships.⏎⏎⏎⏎The RFI essentially makes HHS act like a marketing arm for the AI vendor industry. When federal reimbursement signals and regulatory fast-lanes are given to a new technology category before we have basic interoperability standards in place, the vendors who win those initial contracts become permanent infrastructure. Once embedded into daily clinical workflows, proprietary data formats make removing them practically impossible. The FTC's 2023 report on generative AI and market concentration noted this exact issue, finding that early regulatory entrenchment ends up eliminating the long-term competitive pressure that usually keeps vendor behavior in check.⏎⏎⏎⏎Based on our Q1 2025 internal contract analysis covering 47 health system clients, we observed that reimbursement criteria from payers for non-device AI tools fluctuated across up to 11 separate frameworks within a single regional market. The average compliance overhead per product deployment ran around $340,000. Mid-tier AI developers are usually the ones making the specialized tools for specific patient populations, but they simply cannot afford parallel compliance tracks at those price points. Large incumbents can. I spend a lot of time reviewing these vendor agreements, and the variation in security questionnaires alone is staggering, which drives that overhead cost. Pushing for acceleration without standardizing things first just closes off the market to new entrants.⏎⏎⏎⏎Health economists evaluating EHR vendor dynamics have repeatedly shown that high switching costs in health technology markets completely stifle competition. The ONC's 2024 annual report to Congress acknowledged that interoperability barriers in health IT persist today mostly because early market entrants were never required to build their systems for data portability.⏎⏎⏎⏎HHS needs to impose binding interoperability mandates with real enforcement mechanisms, along with standardized payer reimbursement frameworks, before moving forward with any AI acceleration agenda. Independent market oversight needs to happen prior to acceleration rather than trying to figure it out after the fact. Our regulatory affairs team is available to submit the detailed contract documentation supporting the compliance overhead figures I mentioned. Contact: trivera@komodohealth.com or policy@komodohealth.com.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0066♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Williams♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian Williams⏎4417 Greystone Drive⏎Ellicott City, MD 21043⏎⏎October 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎⏎After my long-time heart doctor left the practice, I spent nearly eight months being managed almost entirely by a computer-flagging system that nobody in the office could explain to me. The nurse practitioner who saw me kept adjusting my beta blocker dosage based on whatever the software recommended, and when I asked why the recommendations hadn't changed even though my symptoms had, she didn't have a clear answer. When I finally got in front of a cardiologist, she found a medication interaction issue within fifteen minutes that the software had apparently been missing the whole time.⏎⏎What I want HHS to understand is that hospitals answer to boards and bottom lines, and when a technology promises to cut costs, administrators cut costs. That means fewer nurses and bigger caseloads and clinicians who are rushed and just go with whatever the algorithm says because it's easier. The people writing the rules for these systems should not be the same people who profit from using them.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Brian Williams♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0067♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Hall♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife Dolores spent six weeks convinced she had a serious illness because a computer flagged her incorrectly, and we had to fight through three appointments to get a human to say otherwise. That experience taught me something. In 2022 there were news reports about a major insurer, UnitedHealth, using an algorithm to deny nursing home stays to elderly patients at a rate something like ten times higher than their own doctors recommended. The people getting hurt by that weren't people who could easily file an appeal. They were 80 years old, or disabled, or didn't speak English, and the process for fighting back was designed to wear them out. I taught seventh grade for 31 years and the first thing I told every student was check your work before you turn it in. HHS should require that a real accountable human being sign off on every prior authorization denial, meaning every decision to refuse to pay for care a doctor ordered, and if HHS chooses not to require that, I'd ask them to be honest that they're choosing to let this continue.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0068♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Walker♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔UnitedHealth Group♔♔♔♔♔♔UnitedHealth Group appreciates the opportunity to share our insights on the Department's RFI focusing on artificial intelligence.⏎⏎⏎⏎The current RFI framing assumes accelerated AI adoption automatically benefits the health system without examining who actually retains the resulting financial savings. Standalone software vendors are increasingly focusing on replacing administrative staff and automating Medicare Advantage coverage determinations under 42 CFR § 422.101 without adequate medical director oversight. HHS must maintain a level regulatory environment by requiring organizations to submit reporting on workforce transition impacts before expanding federal subsidies for these tools. This specific reporting will verify that new efficiencies actually support clinical personnel.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0069♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel♔Nguyen♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔The government has civil rights rules against discrimination in healthcare. So you need to test these new computer programs before letting clinics use them. I am 75 years old and still work retail here in Omaha and I see how things are changing. My regular heart doctor retired back in 2022. Since then I go to the same clinic for my chest pain but I have seen maybe eight different doctors. Every single time it is a new person who just looks at my chart on the screen for ten minutes and agrees with whatever the computer tells them. The human connection in medicine is already going away.⏎⏎Did the companies even test these programs on older Vietnamese men like me? If the data is bad then the machine will make mistakes on certain groups of people. You need to require tests to see if the programs do worse for older people or minorities or non-English speakers before they get approved. The public needs to be allowed to see those test results too. It is confusing why the department would let everyone use these systems without checking if they are fair first.⏎⏎I hear people say that putting more of these systems in hospitals will make healthcare equal for everybody. Anyone who believes that is fooling themselves. People with money and private insurance will get the computer advice and also a real doctor who has time to double check it. For the rest of us on Medicare or Medicaid, the computer will just be the whole doctor. Clinics that are short on cash will just use it so they do not have to pay staff.⏎⏎Do not approve this too fast. The appointments are already getting shorter every year. If you move too fast before having rules to keep patients safe, you are deciding that poorer people will suffer when the computers mess up. Slow down and actually test this to make sure it is safe.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0070♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Campbell♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔IBM Corporation♔♔♔♔♔♔IBM Corporation submits these comments on behalf of Stephanie Campbell, Chief Medical Officer, drawing on experience advising health system clients across more than 40 deployment environments on AI integration and clinical governance. Our central concern is not with AI adoption as such, but with the incentive structures already shaping how health systems are actually choosing to use these tools. In our experience, the deployment decisions getting made right now are far more likely to originate in CFO offices than clinical leadership, and they are oriented toward headcount reduction and productivity monitoring rather than care improvement. We acknowledge that these technologies can genuinely alleviate provider fatigue in specific scenarios, but our implementation metrics more routinely show an uptick in charting requirements, coupled with the systemic displacement of clerical positions staffed predominantly by individuals from historically underrepresented demographics.⏎⏎IBM Corporation | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 2⏎⏎er, with economic gains accruing to executives and vendors rather than the workforce or patients. HHS should require, as a condition of any AI-related reimbursement incentive, that health systems submit independent workforce impact assessments and demonstrate that clinical governance review occurred prior to deployment, not after contract execution.⏎⏎stephanie.campbell@ibm.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0071♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Robinson♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔American Osteopathic Association♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Osteopathic Association strongly advises HHS to mandate direct human oversight for any AI-driven claims decisions prior to expanding artificial intelligence across insurance functions. Although the assertion that artificial intelligence lessens provider fatigue holds true in certain situations, our members more frequently encounter heavier charting demands coupled with the gradual elimination of clerical jobs predominantly occupied by female and minority staff.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0072♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Young♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I was at my cardiologist for a routine follow-up recently, and she told me a software program had already flagged something on my echocardiogram before she even looked at the pictures. I questioned her about where the blame falls if the program overlooks a problem or points her in an incorrect direction. She didn't really have a good answer for me. That really bothered me.⏎⏎I'm 61 and I've worked as an administrative assistant handling records and scheduling at a cardiology office in New Hampshire for twenty years. Because of my job, I see how new software actually gets rolled out. It's always rushed to save money, and there is way less oversight than people think.⏎⏎Looking at this HHS proposal, it seems like you are treating AI like a simple IT update. But deciding how much of our healthcare should be handed over to computers is a massive moral issue. It shouldn't be pushed through by an agency just because tech companies want it done fast. Voters should be deciding this. Your proposal asks for ways to accelerate artificial intelligence adoption in healthcare, taking for granted that moving faster is inherently positive. I don't get why everyone accepts that as a given.⏎⏎My biggest worry is that once we start this, we won't be able to stop it. I remember switching over to electronic health records. Once the new programs were tied to our daily work and our billing, we were stuck with them forever, even when they caused huge headaches. The same thing will happen here. If Medicare and Medicaid start paying for AI diagnoses, it will be impossible to get rid of them later. No politician is going to roll that back, even if we find out years from now that the AI is hurting patients.⏎⏎There are just too many unanswered questions. We don't have the right rules in place. Just as I brought up with my own physician, there is zero clarity on who faces legal liability when a machine learning error causes patient harm. We also know these computer programs can be biased, and they usually end up giving worse advice for patients who already struggle to get good care. People love to say "the doctor makes the final call," but I see how busy doctors are. When a physician has to click through forty computer alerts every morning just to do their job, they eventually start agreeing with whatever the screen says to save time.⏎⏎We need to decide if we want healthcare to be run by actual humans. We need a massive national conversation about this before federal agencies alter the reimbursement structures in ways we can never undo.⏎⏎The Department of Health and Human Services must hit the brakes. Don't rush this. Before pushing more artificial intelligence into the insurance side of things, the department absolutely must ensure that real people double-check any computer-generated choices about what gets covered. Establish firm regulations and create independent review panels funded entirely outside the software sector prior to allowing these algorithms anywhere near medical treatments or insurance claims. Deciding the future of medicine shouldn't be treated like buying new office equipment. I honestly doubt this agency possesses the legal power to make such sweeping determinations for everyone just from reading public comments. We need a real public debate.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0073♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Torres♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My pharmacist mentioned something about new transparency rules and AI, and honestly I'm not sure this is even the right place to ask, but HHS should require proof AI helps patients before any hospital rolls it out. You're trying to figure out how to speed up artificial intelligence in medicine, acting like forcing this stuff through is a guaranteed win. I asked her who's responsible if the computer misses something important or points her in the wrong direction. I honestly don't think the health department possesses the legal power to make this decision for all of us based on a simple comment period. That's something we need to talk out before the feds rewrite the payment regulations and lock them in stone.⏎The agency needs to slow way down. Like I brought up at my own clinic, we don't even know who ends up in court if a software glitch hurts someone. Put strict rules in place and create independent review panels that aren't bankrolled by Silicon Valley before allowing these programs near medical treatments or insurance claims.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0074♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Martinez♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔Sickle Cell Disease Association of America♔♔♔♔♔♔Sickle Cell Disease Association of America, Massachusetts Chapter⏎⏎Boston, MA⏎⏎⏎⏎September 12, 2023⏎⏎⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎⏎⏎Re: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎⏎The Sickle Cell Disease Association of America Massachusetts Chapter is submitting comments on this RFI. We have observed the consolidation of electronic health records over the past fifteen years. We worry AI adoption will follow a similar path where early federal backing leads to vendors becoming permanently entrenched. For patients experiencing acute sickle cell pain crises, underperforming clinical technology is a severe barrier to timely care.⏎⏎Once artificial intelligence platforms become embedded into hospital administration and patient intake operations, the transition costs to replace those systems are prohibitively expensive. Hospitals will avoid changing vendors because it requires overhauling staff training protocols and renegotiating service agreements. This traps providers with their initial vendor, making accountability difficult. To prevent this, HHS must establish open interoperability standards and mandatory vendor portability conditions prior to accelerating the adoption of these tools.⏎⏎For questions, contact Melissa Martinez, Executive Director, at mmartinez@sicklecelldisease-ma.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0075♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Campbell♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Seeing the government try to fast-track artificial intelligence in healthcare scares me to death. My husband Gerald died of small-cell carcinoma. We lost 11 whole months because he was misdiagnosed before a specialist finally figured out what was wrong. 11 months is a long time when you have cancer. I know people want faster diagnosis and I understand that. I really do. But rushing into using artificial intelligence is just not the answer.⏎⏎When Gerald was finally getting his treatments the nurses and doctors just followed whatever the computer told them to do. His actual physical symptoms didn't even match what the computer assumed. Getting a doctor to just look at him, really look at him, and admit the machine might be wrong about a 73-year-old man was incredibly frustrating. They trusted the screen way more than the human being sitting right in front of them in the hospital gown. This makes me sick to my stomach seeing HHS talk about rushing more AI into hospitals. Regular citizens haven't even had a say in this. It shouldn't be up to tech companies working on some fast timeline to decide our care.⏎⏎If you start paying hospitals to use AI we are never going to be able to stop it. Everyone knows that once hospitals start doing something new they never stop. Just look at those electronic records on the laptops they bring in the room. Doctors spend the whole appointment typing into those things now instead of looking at the patient. We all complain about it but we are stuck with them. If you push AI into clinics before there are rules the public will have no power. Once the hospitals depend on it to get paid it will be too expensive to change even if patients get hurt. We won't be able to turn it off.⏎⏎The health department really has to pump the brakes right now. Who decided rushing this was a good idea? When did patients agree to this? We need a way for the public to actually weigh in before you start giving out money to use AI. There has to be a way to turn these systems off if they hurt people instead of just hoping for the best, and the safety rules need to be written today not as some afterthought once the money is already handed out.⏎⏎Medicine is supposed to be about taking care of people. Patients should have a voice. Right now these decisions are being made by people in offices who aren't the ones actually lying in the hospital beds worried about their lives.⏎⏎Dorothy Campbell⏎⏎Tucson, AZ♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0076♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Young♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔American Hospital Association♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of our member hospitals, the American Hospital Association is providing these comments on the topic of AI-assisted care. We are concerned that the populations most frequently encountering these tools have the least ability to question the source of their medical advice. Patients in underserved communities frequently do not know when a treatment plan is drafted by a predictive model rather than the physician in the room. The Department must determine whether the push for implementation is truly benefiting these individuals or merely creating operational efficiencies for the facilities using the technology.⏎ HHS should prioritize clinical safety over accelerated deployment timelines. Patients rely on medical professionals for independent clinical judgment and direct accountability, which are core elements of medical practice that software cannot replicate.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0077♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel♔Adams♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔University of Colorado Anschutz Medical Campus♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel Adams, Postdoctoral Fellow, University of Colorado Anschutz Medical Campus, respectfully submits that HHS cannot credibly claim fidelity to its equity mandates while accelerating AI adoption without first requiring demographic performance stratification across vulnerable populations.⏎Marginalized individuals rarely realize when algorithms, not clinicians, design their care.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0078♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Lopez♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Back in 2022 my mom was in a hospital here in Albuquerque for three weeks. It happened because some automated heart monitor system got her condition wrong. She is a 68-year-old Chicana. We found out later that the computer program wasn't really tested on people like her. I work in retail, I'm not a doctor or a lawyer. But dealing with all that put me in meetings with hospital bosses and insurance people, and it made me see how these choices are getting made.⏎⏎⏎⏎I was trying to read the actual notice for this rule online and it is just too confusing. It is full of legal jargon. If regular people who actually have to deal with these medical systems can't even figure out what you are asking, then the public isn't really getting a say. It just feels like this is only set up for hospital lobbyists to reply.⏎⏎⏎⏎The notice talks a lot about speeding things up with AI, but you have to look at who actually benefits from that. The money and time saved by using AI in clinics mostly just goes to hospital bosses who want to fire office workers. It goes to insurance companies so they can use computers to automatically deny claims. And it helps the tech companies who already have government contracts. When you look at who actually gets laid off because of this tech, it is usually the female and minority staff doing all the clerical stuff. I read a report from the nurses union in 2023 that showed these AI tools aren't even helping tired nurses. They are just being used to track how fast the nurses are working and get them in trouble if they slow down. That isn't being efficient, it is just spying on workers. I don't think your agency is really thinking about that part.⏎⏎⏎⏎People keep saying AI is going to fix doctor burnout. I really doubt it. I have been through enough new computer system rollouts at my retail job to know that when a new tech tool is forced on you, it usually just makes your job harder. If a nurse or a doctor thinks the AI is wrong, they probably don't have the time or the power to argue with the computer. So they just go along with it, and the patient is the one who gets hurt in the end.⏎⏎⏎⏎Before the government pays for any of these AI systems, you need to force them to do a real study on how it affects the workers. And this study needs to be done by an outside group, not just the company selling the software. They need to show exactly what jobs are getting cut, who is working in those jobs, and where those people are supposed to go. Also, the government needs to make a rule that nurses and doctors are allowed to disagree with the AI without getting in trouble with their bosses. They need a safe way to speak up. That is just basic worker protection.⏎⏎⏎⏎I know I don't know all the fancy policy words to answer every single question you have. But I know how businesses work. Right now all the rewards are going to the wrong people, and the patients and regular workers are the ones paying for it.⏎⏎⏎⏎David Lopez⏎⏎⏎⏎Albuquerque, New Mexico♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0079♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Nguyen♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My husband Thanh was diagnosed with stage three pancreatic cancer in March of 2019, eight months after the first time we sat in an exam room and described his symptoms to a physician who was, I now understand, pattern-matching against the most common presentations of the most common conditions. He did not fit those patterns. He was sixty-one, Vietnamese American, non-smoker, healthy weight, and the pain was in a place and of a character that apparently did not trigger the right flags in the diagnostic flow. I have spent the years since going back through those records the way I go through reference materials at work, carefully, without hurrying, looking for where the system failed and where the people inside the system failed and where those two things were actually the same failure.⏎⏎What I want to say to HHS is this: the thing that finally caught it was not a test or an algorithm. It was a specialist at UCHealth in Aurora who watched Thanh sit down in the chair and noticed something in how he moved, something about the way he was protecting his right side without seeming to know he was doing it. She asked him to describe the pain using his own words, not a scale of one to ten, not a checklist. She sat with the silence after he answered. That is the part that stays with me. The silence. She was reading something that had no field in any electronic health record I have ever seen.⏎⏎I understand that HHS is asking about how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings and what regulatory or reimbursement changes might help. I am not opposed to AI in medicine in any blanket way, I want to say that clearly. What concerns me is a specific confusion between speed and accuracy that I watched cost my husband eight months, and whether AI adoption, especially when tied to reimbursement incentives, will pressure clinicians to move faster through exactly the kind of ambiguous, atypical presentations where slower, more relational attention is what makes the difference.⏎⏎There is research, as Kelley and her co-authors have documented, showing that the quality of the relationship between patient and clinician independently predicts health outcomes and adherence, separate from the technical quality of the medical assessment or care strategy. That relationship is not a soft amenity. It is doing clinical work. When we build systems that route around it in the name of efficiency, we are not just losing something warm and pleasant, we are losing something that has measurable effect.⏎⏎Here are a few points I hope the department will consider, imperfectly worded because I am not a clinician or a policy person:⏎⏎1. Any reimbursement changes tied to AI adoption should include explicit protections for visit time, not just diagnostic accuracy metrics.⏎⏎2. Atypical presentations, which are often more common in patients of color and in older patients, should be specifically studied in any AI validation process before deployment.⏎⏎3. Human clinician judgment should remain the documented decision-maker, not a co-signer for the algorithm's output.⏎⏎Thanh died in January 2022. I keep the records in a folder I labeled with his name and the year we first went looking for answers. I do not know what to do with that folder, and I am not sure this comment will change anything, but it felt wrong not to send it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0080♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Robinson♔♔California♔♔United States♔NextGen Healthcare♔♔♔♔♔♔NextGen Healthcare formally recommends that the Department mandate the inclusion of therapeutic relationship impact assessments within any clinical AI evaluation framework, rather than focusing solely on technical performance metrics, prior to accelerating adoption.⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎⏎Published literature by Kelley and other researchers clearly documents that the strength of the bond between patient and provider is an independent predictor of health outcomes and medication adherence, entirely distinct from the clinical accuracy of a specific medical assessment or intervention protocol. If we design technological workflows that bypass this human connection simply to maximize throughput, we are sacrificing more than just bedside manner; we are actively degrading a component with proven medical efficacy. This underlying reality remains my primary focus as a medical officer. While my perspective is rooted in clinical leadership rather than direct regulatory drafting, NextGen requests that the Department consider the following parameters: 1.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0081♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Rodriguez♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My patient in 2019 got two days of wrong treatment because an automated system didn't recognize her presentation as diabetic crisis, and she didn't fit whatever demographic profile the algorithm expected. That's the quiet version of what's happening. The louder version is insurance companies running AI denial engines that reject claims faster than any human being could read them, and the patients on the receiving end — elderly, disabled, poor, non-English-speaking — just stop fighting. HHS has the clear authority to require actual people to evaluate these denials, and deciding not to use that leverage is a deliberate choice.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0082♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Robinson♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To Whom It May Concern at HHS,⏎My friend Dave, a fellow veteran from our unit in Pennsylvania, spent the better part of 2019 getting bounced between providers who kept writing "anxiety" on his chart before anyone caught his TBI. What stands out to me is this: a computer system running on cost-reduction logic would have done the exact same thing, just faster, leaving him with zero human beings to challenge when things went sideways. Medical facilities and insurance companies are just waiting to grab any approved algorithm to slash nursing and support jobs, directly hitting the female and minority staff members who actually provide hands-on care. It baffles me that the department is willing to push ahead with this without demanding hard evidence that it actually helps patients before letting these systems go live.⏎Robert Robinson⏎Pittsburgh, PA♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0083♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Rodriguez♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔American Diabetes Association♔♔♔♔♔♔Our national diabetes advocacy organization strongly recommends that Health and Human Services mandate substantive personnel oversight for all machine-generated coverage denials before expanding computerized pre-approval networks that are already harming vulnerable patients at documented scale.⏎⏎It is difficult to comprehend why the agency would advance these rules without requiring concrete evidence of patient benefit before authorizing any deployment. Medical facilities and health plans will inevitably leverage any sanctioned algorithmic tools to justify reducing nursing and administrative staff, disproportionately impacting the female and minority workers who provide the actual care. The core issue remains clear: an algorithmic platform driven by cost-containment parameters would execute the exact same denials, but at a greater velocity, and leaving affected individuals with zero recourse when contesting those decisions.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0084♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Mitchell♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was misdiagnosed TWICE before anyone caught her ovarian cancer early enough to do something about it, and both times it came down to a doctor who was too rushed to look at the full picture. So when I hear that HHS wants to move FASTER on putting AI into diagnosis rooms, it leaves me feeling extremely anxious. I read somewhere that AI tools were denying insurance claims for elderly patients without any real review, and I remember reading about pulse oximeters that didn't work right on darker skin for years before anyone said anything!! My biggest question is honestly just -- is this already decided?? I thought this was a request for comments but the language in some places sounds like it's already happening. I'm not a lawyer or a doctor, just a librarian in Illinois who wants someone at HHS to please slow down and make sure we get this right before expanding the use of algorithms to approve or deny medical care. As leading patient advocacy groups for diabetics have pointed out, the health department needs to mandate that a real person checks every single AI coverage rejection. Rushing these computer systems is a bad idea when there is already proof they are hurting at-risk patients.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0085♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Garcia♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔Society for Public Health Education♔♔♔♔♔♔Writing on behalf of the Society for Public Health Education, HHS must assess who actually profits from hasty AI implementation before automating the administrative and medical coding roles heavily staffed by female and minority personnel across American healthcare.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0086♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Adams♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔Weil Cornell Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Weill Cornell Medicine's Population Health Sciences division urges HHS to condition any acceleration of clinical AI adoption on mandatory prospective equity impact assessments stratified by patient population, income level, and care setting.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0087♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Campbell♔♔Virginia♔♔United States♔Society for Imaging Informatics in Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔The Society for Imaging Informatics in Medicine formally recommends that HHS link all AI reimbursement incentives directly to demonstrated prospective real-world validation across diverse patient populations, rather than relying on single-institution benchmarks.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0088♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Sanchez♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔National Association of Social Workers♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the National Association of Social Workers, HHS must halt the acceleration of AI vendor consolidation before safety-net clinics lose whatever remaining leverage they currently hold.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0089♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Anderson♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔After my regular VA heart specialist left the agency, I spent roughly eight months getting bounced around remote video appointments and generic checklists while my actual arterial blockage went totally unnoticed. When the VA finally brought in an AI diagnostic tool to cover the staffing gap, it flagged me for a condition I didn't have. My new doctor caught the real issue on her first visit with a stethoscope. Someone made a budget decision and called it a care decision. Before HHS accelerates any of this, I want a plain answer: does this rule apply to Tricare and VA beneficiaries, or just commercial patients? My family deserves to know whether these tools are coming for us too.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0090♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Green♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My brother Cale had a bone infection in his foot for over a year before anyone caught it. Two Tulsa urgent care clinics, fourteen months, and the system just kept moving him along. I experienced the exact same thing when my heart doctor left the VA system a while back, spending eight straight months getting bounced between remote video screens and generic advice while a serious arterial issue went completely missed. I don't bring that up to make this personal. I bring it up because when HHS asks about fast-tracking these algorithms into clinical care, I want to know who is accountable when the next Cale falls through the cracks and the reason on the chart is "the algorithm flagged it low priority."⏎That is the question this RFI is not asking. It is asking how to accelerate. It is not asking who takes the blame when rushing things causes harm.⏎I work construction. I understand liability chains. When a scaffold collapses on a jobsite, we do not spend time asking how to build faster scaffolds. We ask who signed off, who inspected, who carries the insurance. Healthcare AI right now has none of that sorted out. W. Nicholson Price II and colleagues wrote in JAMA back in 2019 that existing tort frameworks do not cleanly assign liability when an AI system contributes to a clinical harm. That was six years ago and I have not seen anything from HHS that fixes it.⏎What I picture happening, once these systems get baked into the EHR and the billing codes, is this: the vendor walks away clean because the contract says the tool is just a decision support aid. The hospital points at the contract. The doctor says the algorithm told me so. And the patient, or the patient's family, is left holding nothing.⏎That is not a hypothetical. That is the logical structure of every AI deployment I have read about in clinical settings. The liability gets diffused until it disappears entirely.⏎And here is what scares me about acceleration specifically. Once a system is integrated into the workflow, once the billing codes are written around it, once the EHR vendor has a license agreement that assumes its presence, you cannot pull it out even when you know it is hurting people. The lock-in is not just technical. It is contractual and financial and bureaucratic. I have seen enough construction projects to know that fixing a structural problem after the concrete is poured costs ten times what it would have cost to design it right.⏎It baffles me that HHS is pushing for a faster rollout instead of focusing on accountability first. I am not saying don't adopt AI. I am saying don't adopt it in a way that makes accountability impossible to assign after the fact.⏎My supplemental insurance plan went up 22% this year and nobody at HHS seems interested in explaining that either, but that is a separate issue I will leave aside.⏎The one thing I want HHS to take away from this comment is that speed without accountability is not progress. Before any regulatory action to accelerate adoption, there needs to be a clear, non-disclaimable framework that identifies a responsible party when AI-assisted care causes harm. Not a safe harbor. Not a voluntary best practice. A named, identifiable, legally non-transferable obligation.⏎Right now the question of who pays when it goes wrong has no good answer, and I'd like to know why that didn't come first.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0091♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Moore♔♔Ohio♔♔United States♔American Association of Naturopathic Physicians♔♔♔♔♔♔American Association of Naturopathic Physicians Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎⏎⏎The AANP finds it baffling that HHS prioritizes rapid deployment over accountability. Instead of asking how to hasten technology integration in clinical settings, HHS must address liability for algorithmic harm. We urge requiring meaningful informed consent and bias auditing protocols first.⏎⏎⏎⏎American Association of Naturopathic Physicians Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0092♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Clark♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔DeepScribe♔♔♔♔♔♔DeepScribe submits these observations with concern that the acceleration framing adopted by HHS may replicate the vendor lock-in dynamics that made EHR consolidation so costly for healthcare institutions. If interoperability requirements and vendor-neutral standards are not finalized beforehand, government payment programs are just going to finance market capture for a handful of massive technology corporations. Our compliance experience suggests switching costs alone will make those vendor relationships nearly permanent.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0093♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Thomas♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔UnidosUS♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of UnidosUS, HHS must prioritize health equity and informed consent protections before accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings serving Latino and low-income communities across Michigan and nationally. To start, utilizing federal payment structures to drive uptake funds monopolies for massive tech corporations. Furthermore, our compliance background shows that once safety-net clinics are locked into a system, the costs to migrate later become practically insurmountable.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0094♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Walker♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔National Disability Rights Network♔♔♔♔♔♔Writing for the National Disability Rights Network, I call on HHS to mandate human oversight for any AI-driven benefit decisions prior to expanding the use of these algorithms in the insurance sector. Disabled, elderly, and low-income patients are already losing medically necessary care to automated denials they cannot meaningfully challenge.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0095♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Nelson♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔University of Wisconsin School of Medicine and Public Health♔♔♔♔♔♔The University of Wisconsin School of Medicine and Public Health respectfully submits that expanding artificial intelligence without first addressing systemic accountability gaps will leave harmed patients without meaningful recourse.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0096♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Davis♔♔Kentucky♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thirty-two years in cardiac care taught me that when a system keeps producing the same bad outcome and nobody changes it, that's not a malfunction, that's a choice, and what always frustrates me about these AI denial systems is that HHS has the absolute power to demand a real human signature on every coverage denial, yet they are choosing not to use it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0097♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Walker♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My father was misdiagnosed three years in a row at a regional clinic outside Pierre. The correct diagnosis eventually came not from any system or technology but from his GP in Brookings noticing a discrepancy in a paper chart during a routine review. I think about that a lot when I read documents like this one.⏎⏎⏎⏎I manage administrative processes for a living. I am not opposed to technology. What I am is familiar with what happens when you optimize a workflow before you understand what the workflow is actually supposed to produce. You get something faster. Whether you get something better is a separate question, and in my experience it often doesn't get asked until after the system is already running.⏎⏎⏎⏎The question this RFI is really asking, though I don't think it's framed this way, is whether HHS should use its regulatory, reimbursement, and research levers simultaneously to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care before the governance infrastructure to govern that adoption exists. I think the answer to that question should be no, and I want to be specific about why.⏎⏎⏎⏎Studies exist—such as the work by Ash's team concerning general health IT—showing how clinical software stays rooted in medical environments well beyond the time its actual usefulness would warrant ripping it out. No major EHR system has ever been successfully pulled out of a large health system after deployment regardless of how it performed. The political and contractual costs of removal always exceed the costs of tolerating whatever the system is doing wrong. That is not a technology problem. It is a governance problem, and it is a predictable one.⏎⏎⏎⏎AI embedded in reimbursement codes will be harder to dislodge than an EHR, not easier. Once vendors are inside the billing infrastructure, the political cost of removing them will reliably exceed the political cost of accepting known harms. That asymmetry is not hypothetical. It is how these markets work. The ratchet only moves one direction.⏎⏎⏎⏎I want to say something that I don't see said often in these comments. A society is entitled to decide that medicine should be slower, more accountable, and more human, even at some measurable efficiency cost. That is a legitimate values choice. It is not a failure of imagination or a resistance to progress. The question of how much AI belongs in clinical decisionmaking is a democratic and moral question, and I am genuinely uncertain that an administrative agency should answer it unilaterally on behalf of an acceleration agenda, particularly when the decision, once baked into payment models and day-to-day medical procedures, cannot be practically unmade.⏎⏎⏎⏎Here is what the department needs to resolve before advancing any of the specific regulatory and payment actions this RFI considers:⏎⏎⏎⏎1. What democratic process established that acceleration is the public's preference?⏎⏎2. What is the exit mechanism if AI-assisted diagnostics produce systematic errors in underserved populations?⏎⏎3. Who bears liability when a reimbursed AI recommendation is wrong?⏎⏎⏎⏎I don't think those are rhetorical questions. I think they are the questions that have to be answered before you open the highway, not after traffic is already moving at speed.⏎⏎⏎⏎My father got the right diagnosis eventually. But only because one physician slowed down long enough to look at what the system had been producing for three years. I'm not sure the system being proposed here has room for that kind of review built into it anywhere, and I'm not sure what the plan is if it turns out we needed it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0098♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty♔Ramirez♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Page 1 of 1⏎⏎My doc told me in January 2023 that the echocar diogram software "wasn't trained on women my age" -- I'm 68, a veteran, been seen at the Rockford clinic since my cardiologist retired. That stuck with me. The administration will just use these programs to hand out pink slips to the nursing and billing departments, hitting minority and female workers the hardest. Who profits? Not us.⏎⏎HHS should ask:⏎⏎1. Who actually benefits when a hospital deploys AI⏎⏎2. What happens to the worker whose job it replaces⏎⏎Fax transmission complete♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0099♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Wilson♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔American College of Physicians♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted on behalf of the American College of Physicians, this document responds to the HHS inquiry regarding AI implementation in clinical environments.⏎⏎WHO ABSORBS THE BURDEN⏎⏎The current framing approaches the issue in reverse. Health systems and insurers gain financially when algorithms automate coverage denials and reduce administrative roles. The workforce and patients ultimately suffer the consequences.⏎⏎HHS must recalibrate its core premise. Rather than looking for ways to accelerate adoption, the department must prioritize establishing robust safety nets for individuals without institutional leverage who are harmed by adverse algorithmic outcomes.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0100♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Thompson♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔Wake Forest School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Writing as a Postdoctoral Fellow at Wake Forest School of Medicine, I, Matthew Thompson, offer this feedback out of profound concern. Instead of focusing on expediting implementation, HHS must ask what safety nets protect those with minimal institutional influence in the event of algorithmic errors.⏎⏎The ratchet effect is real. Once AI tools are embedded in federal reimbursement structures, reversing course will cost more politically than tolerating known harms.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0101♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Davis♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔Agfa HealthCare♔♔♔♔♔♔Agfa HealthCare urges HHS to mandate real-world performance validation standards and staged deployment requirements prior to scaling reimbursement incentives for clinical AI tools.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0102♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Rivera♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔Society of Hospital Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Society of Hospital Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎The Society of Hospital Medicine recommends that the Department firmly dismiss this accelerationist framing. Patients need human presence, not faster processing.⏎⏎Society of Hospital Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0103♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔Lewis♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My neighbor Dot, eighty-three years old, went through three misdiagnoses over eighteen months before a young hospitalist actually sat down with her paper chart and caught that her symptoms pointed to a rare adrenal condition. Not anxiety. Not old age. An adrenal condition. I spent forty years as a bedside nurse and I know what gets lost when you stop watching a person's hands, stop noticing that a family member went quiet, stop reading the room. Those things are the diagnosis, not decoration around it. Reading this proposal, it feels like the government thinks rushing through care is somehow a good thing, and I simply cannot grasp why. You absolutely must demand that a specific, real-life doctor or nurse answers for any choice made with these computer tools. Not a department. Not a system. A person whose license is on the line if Dot gets sent home with the wrong answer again.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0104♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Harris♔♔Kansas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Two years back my potassium dropped to a dangerous level and an automated message told me to "follow up as needed." Nobody called. I waited three weeks. HHS acting like rushing is good makes zero sense. Someone needs to stay accountable when these systems flag something serious, because slowing down to keep a real person responsible for that moment is worth more than any efficiency gain.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0105♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Green♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔Johns Hopkins Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Johns Hopkins Medicine | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 1⏎⏎On behalf of Johns Hopkins Medicine, I am offering our feedback on the HHS request for information regarding the acceleration of AI integration into clinical care. Our remarks draw on extensive operational experience across a major academic health system, alongside a 2024 analysis facilitated by FastHealth Corporation's network of 214 partner hospital systems. That analysis determined 67% of financial officers point to inconsistent payer standards as the primary barrier to expanding AI-enabled tools, given that average compliance preparation costs exceed $340,000 per new deployment. We have watched this pattern before. EHR rollouts, sepsis prediction algorithms, clinical decision support alerts — each arrived with compelling benchmark data and each, once deployed across real hospital environments with fragmented, inconsistently coded records, performed materially worse than the demonstrations suggested. What concerns our organization is not AI in principle but the familiar acceleration before that reliability gap is understood.⏎⏎Johns Hopkins Medicine | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 2⏎⏎Real clinical environments are far noisier than any vendor demonstration assumes, and when clinicians begin trusting AI outputs, there is a genuine operational risk that their own vigilance decreases at precisely the moments the system is most likely to be wrong. HHS should require independent, real-world performance validation across varied patient populations and EHR configurations before expanding deployment pathways, rather than after.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0106♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Taylor♔♔New York♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS has to stop rushing this AI initiative. We need actual oversight before these programs are everywhere.⏎⏎My sister spent three months back in 2022 getting bounced between different specialists for an autoimmune condition. An automated screening tool at her clinic decided she was low-risk. Because her doctors were totally slammed and rushed, nobody even questioned the software. They just followed what the screen said and ignored her actual symptoms.⏎⏎I manage databases for a living at a library in NY, so I know firsthand how easily bad system outputs get taken as absolute truth by users. When you put that same dynamic into a medical office with overworked staff, it's a disaster waiting to happen. Nobody asked my sister or any other patient if they wanted some random software making calls in their medical chart. The clinic just bought it and turned it on one day. Once these systems are running, trying to argue with the computer's output as a patient is basically impossible.⏎⏎Please slow down. You need to listen to actual regular people and get our input, not just talk to your corporate partners, before making any final decisions on this.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0107♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Walker♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I have worked in a warehouse down here in Florida for over thirty years. I'm used to my back hurting, but two years ago my chest started hurting bad. I went to an ER in Orlando. The triage nurse punched what I was feeling into their system, and the program decided I could wait. So they left me in the waiting room for four hours. It was a real heart attack. I only survived because an older nurse walked by, saw how gray I looked, and completely ignored what the screen was telling her. My physician later explained that the reason the computer missed my heart attack is because those systems aren't very good at recognizing warning signs in older women. Whoever built the software probably just tested it on younger guys.⏎⏎⏎⏎Since then I've been trying to figure out how something like that is allowed. I was reading some articles online about medical lawsuits and AI, and the scariest part is that nobody seems to know who is actually liable. If I had died in that waiting room chair, my kids wouldn't even know who to go after. The hospital would just say they were following the software. But you can't drag a computer program into court. And the tech company that sold it probably has contracts protecting them from getting sued by patients.⏎⏎⏎⏎Now I see HHS is trying to get hospitals to use even more of this AI. It completely baffles me why you are encouraging this when the regulations aren't even finalized yet. Just because some agency approves a software doesn't mean it is safe for a 69-year-old woman. And once a hospital spends millions on a new computer system, they are going to make the staff rely on it. Even if the experienced nurses know the machine is wrong.⏎⏎⏎⏎It is always the patient who ends up paying the price. You shouldn't be pushing this technology into our hospitals when you haven't figured out the most basic problems. Before you encourage anybody to adopt more AI tools, I want you to answer one question. When a computer algorithm screws up and injures somebody, who is the one taken to court? I don't want to hear a complicated legal excuse. I want to know how a regular person gets justice when the machine makes a fatal error. Until you have an answer for that, you need to slow down.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0108♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔White♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔BioNTech♔♔♔♔♔♔BioNTech's administrative operations group warns that deploying AI into reimbursement and clinical workflows before governance frameworks are settled is illogical. When algorithms fail—such as missing cardiac events in older women presenting with acute chest pain—liability remains undefined. Even when nurses spot errors, patients suffer. HHS requires broader deliberation.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
